WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
ENSOscope is a free, open website that turns the seasonal El Niño forecast into something

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.800
you can act on, what it means for your region and how far ahead.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.680
Let me walk through it as if I were a health team in Kenya.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.360
I start here on the Forecast tab.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.180
This tells me in plain words what is coming.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.720
Right now, a strong El Niño, strengthening over the next few months.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.720
The bars show how confident the models are.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.180
No need to know the exact numbers, the message is clear.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:35.300
A big event is likely, so it is worth preparing now.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.200
My next question is, what does that actually mean for us?

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.360
I open Teleconnections.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.800
This is the world map of how a strong El Niño reshapes rainfall everywhere at once.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.360
I leave the source on its default, which says observed rainfall, CHIRPS.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:59.760
This shows simply a map built from real observed rainfall, not a model.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.520
And I want you to look at eastern Africa.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:08.320
That whole region, including Kenya, is where I'm going to focus.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.160
First, rain. I keep Wet selected and switch to the short-rains season, September to November.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.360
On this map, blue means wetter than usual.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.680
East Africa turns blue, so let me zoom into Kenya to see it properly.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.320
I click and the map drills down to just my country.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.400
Now it is clear.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.000
In a strong El Niño, Kenya's short rains usually run wetter, sometimes a lot wetter.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.120
For a health team, that points to a real risk of flooding and the disease risks that

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.400
can follow it.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.960
That is exactly the kind of thing that I want to see coming months ahead.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.520
Now I want to double check that.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.640
Up here, I switch the source from real observations to a climate model.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.160
One that has been run 50 times.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.640
You will see that the pattern over Kenya barely changes.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.320
Still wetter than usual, which is reassuring.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.520
And now these little dots appear.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.760
Think of the dots as a confidence check.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:13.440
The dots show where the many events do not agree on the sign of the change.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.160
With real observations, we only have a handful of past strong El Niño events, far too few

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.200
to measure that kind of agreement.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.160
The model gives us more events, so it can show me where the wetter signal is solid

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.600
or not, and where it is shakier.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.520
When the observations and the model tell the same story, I trust it more.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.520
I can also flip to Drought. This shows the longest run of dry days, still zoomed in on

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.520
Kenya.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:45.120
It is worth a look, because a different season or a phase like La Niña can bring the opposite

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.800
problem, not too much water but too little.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.800
Next, heat.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.800
For this one, I switch back to the observed data because the heat measure isn't in

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:56.800
the model yet.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.580
I choose Heat stress.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:05.440
It uses a number called WBGT, wet bulb globe temperature, which is basically a feels-like

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:08.840
value that combines temperature and humidity.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.120
Redder means more dangerous heat for the body.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.080
Before I act, I ask, can I trust this?

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.760
I open the Skill tab.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.840
This is the model's track record.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.840
The orange line is the forecast, the black line is what actually happened, and you can

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.240
see they move together, almost on top of each other, and these bars show something important.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:35.600
The model tells strong events apart from ordinary ones.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:43.080
At the few-months lead I care about, it is reliable, so I can act with some confidence.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.080
So here is the payoff.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:49.880
Months ahead, I already know that the wetter short rains and a flood risk are likely

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:51.520
for Kenya.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.800
That early warning is the whole point.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.360
Now, that is ENSOscope, from forecast, to your region, to action.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:07.040
It is free and open, and there is a Guide tab, which is the one that is open right now,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.400
that walks anyone through the same steps in plain language.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.200
Thanks for watching.
